{"kind":"report","text":"changes-requested at exact head a5ccd18bcf04c3d50a123687d89715282144e41e.\n\nARCHITECTURE: affected layers are 1 Ordinary Git storage (internal/gitstore) and 2 Kernel (internal/kernel); internal/service is touched only in a test. Contracts are preserved. gitstore already owned SSH signature verification (VerifySSHCommit, ValidateSSHPublicKey) and payload-tree shape (ValidatePayloadTree); AuditBatch adds an in-process implementation of those same storage-layer responsibilities and imports no application schema. Every kernel duty enumerated in docs/reference/architecture.md section 2 (verification of history, object shape, signatures, ordering, payload binding) is still enforced. No contract change, so no same-head architecture update is required. I also grepped docs/, performance/ and notes/ for a recorded per-record subprocess cost that this head would invalidate and found none; docs/reference/limits.md still says only that cold audit cost grows with depth, which remains true.\n\nSECURITY: no defect found. Examined, not taken from the report. (1) Object identity: every byte returned flows through readObject, which recomputes the id with hashObject over the canonical git preimage \"\u003ctype\u003e \u003clen\u003e\\0\u003cpayload\u003e\" for sha1 and sha256 and rejects a mismatch; ReadCommit, readTree, readPayloadBlob and TreeIsEmpty all funnel through it, so no claimed id is trusted. The comparison need not be constant time because the expected id is public. (2) No packfile or index metadata is trusted: git's claimed size is used only for framing and the pre-read ceiling, and an understated or overstated size ends in a hash mismatch or a framing error, so bounds are effectively enforced over hash-verified bytes. (3) Hostile parsing: object ids are validated as exact-length lowercase hex before reaching the batch stream, so no abbreviated lookup or newline injection; a desynchronised stream is poisoned by killing the process rather than resynced; commit parsing rejects a missing separator, duplicate tree, committer or signature, bad continuations and empty header names or values; tree parsing bounds every field (space\u003e0, nul\u003espace+1, len\u003e=nul+1+objectBytes); sshWireString bounds-checks every length prefix and cannot underflow. (4) Signed payload equivalence: SignedContent is the commit bytes with the gpgsig header and its continuation lines removed and every other byte preserved in order, which is exactly what git strips, so the signature covers the same tree, parents, committer, timestamp and message that are reported, and the whole content is bound to the oid by the hash check. (5) SSHSIG verification follows PROTOCOL.sshsig: armor type, magic, version 1, embedded key compared byte-equal to the sequencer key wire form, namespace git, empty reserved, sha256 or sha512 only, ssh-ed25519 only, exact 64-byte signature, no trailing bytes, and the signed blob is MAGIC||namespace||reserved||hash_alg||H(message). Verification uses the expected sequencer key, not the embedded one, and failure aborts the audit. (6) Ceilings: the event blob is bounded by the ceiling and each attachment by ceiling-total, and total can never exceed ceiling, so the uint64 subtraction cannot underflow. (7) Spot-verified invariants that remain enforced: ordering and parents (validateSequenceBounds plus validateChainParents, now stronger because parents come from hash-verified bytes instead of git log output), actor signature (intent.Verify), causal trailers (intent.EqualRefs), signed tree binding (commit.Tree equals decoded.PayloadTree), envelope ceiling, dedup and idempotency conflict, key rotation (the signature is checked with the current key before rotation is applied, rotation to the current key is refused, and the successor key is parsed and validated), and the rotation empty-tree check, where zero-length hash-verified content is equivalent to the empty-tree oid. (8) Cancellation and cleanup: exec.CommandContext plus a deferred Close that surfaces the git-side failure and maps cancellation to ctx.Err().\n\nTwo non-blocking observations. pem.Decode silently skips bytes before the BEGIN marker, so armor with leading garbage is accepted where OpenSSH is stricter; this is not exploitable because the garbage lies in the gpgsig header outside the signed payload, so forging still needs the sequencer key. Commit objects are read with no size limit before the envelope ceiling applies; this is parity with main, whose readCommitMetadata also had no per-message cap, so it is not a regression, but passing the ceiling once desc is known would remove an unbounded allocation on hostile input.\n\nDIFFERENTIAL: I ran both implementations over the live 5831-record gitseq log at the same head 37a2c04aabbbfe6d91c3b2f8b3ae7a2e91e76e3a (ref unchanged before and after). Output was byte-identical: Depth 5831, Events 5831. Old path 213.17s; new path 1.10s with exactly 3 git subprocesses, counted with a PATH shim rather than inferred.\n\nMUTATION SPOT-CHECK: I broke three verification seams in the worktree and all three went red. Neutralising ed25519.Verify turned TestVerifySSHSignatureRejectsChangedInputs/changed_content red; neutralising the object-id comparison turned TestAuditBatchRecomputesObjectHash red; removing the payload shape checks turned the kernel tests TestVerifierRejectsExtraPayloadTreeEntry and TestVerifierRejectsInvalidAttachmentName red, which also proves the kernel hostile suite reaches the new batch path and not only the gitstore unit tests. Each file was restored and the worktree is clean at a5ccd18b.\n\nGATES at the exact head: go build ./... clean, go vet ./... clean, go test ./... -count=1 exit 0 across 25 packages on the first run. BenchmarkColdAuditAtDepth50000 with -benchtime=1x measured 16.53 s/op, 3.000 git-processes and 0.00006 git-processes per record on an Apple M5 Max, comfortably inside the 5-minute target and against a 2.00-per-record baseline that would have been 100,002 processes.\n\nWHY CHANGES ARE REQUESTED: the timing-dependent service test adjustment is not sufficient, and the head is not reliably green. internal/service/server_test.go TestProfileMismatchRebuildIsSingleFlightAndPublishesAtomically fails about one run in four at this head. Measured: go test ./internal/service/ -count=15 gave 4 failures at a5ccd18b, and the same command at main 316d5aeb gave 0 failures in 15. Three failures were at server_test.go:1026, \"status returned before the known mid-scan rebuild finished\", and one at server_test.go:1036 with {Running:false Verified:0 Total:0}. The cause is this change: the fixture is only 30 records, and a roughly 200-fold faster verifier lets the whole rebuild, including fold and publish, finish inside the test's 100ms observation window. Relaxing the later assertion to !stillRunning.Running does not fix it, because the earlier unchanged assertion still has to catch the rebuild mid-verification with 0 \u003c Verified \u003c Total/2, and that window is now a few milliseconds. The repair should make the test's control of rebuild timing deterministic, for example a gate or hook that holds the rebuild while the readers are observed, or a fixture large enough to keep the window real, rather than weakening what is asserted. Merging a head that fails a gate run in four would poison the gate for everyone.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION: loadCommit now carries two implementations of the same payload and rotation checks, selected by batch != nil, because scanAfter still uses the git-subprocess path. That is new divergence surface: two bodies of code must keep enforcing one invariant. I am not asking for it inside this change, since the delta path is warm and short and unifying would widen the scope, but it deserves a follow-up request to move scanAfter onto the batch reader and delete ValidatePayloadTree and the ReadFile loop. No other fluff found; the new code is tight and the removed duplication in ValidateSSHPublicKey is a genuine improvement.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:dc934b75080a43b84f0ee00eb3fb42f1ddd34135","head":"a5ccd18bcf04c3d50a123687d89715282144e41e","verdict":"changes-requested"}}